WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.037
NumPy gives us several convenient functions for creating numeric sequences and placeholder arrays.

00:00:06.037 --> 00:00:12.242
N P dot arange creates values using a start, stop, and optional step.

00:00:12.242 --> 00:00:16.042
In this example, we create the days one through seven.

00:00:16.042 --> 00:00:19.072
N P dot linspace works differently.

00:00:19.072 --> 00:00:26.477
Instead of specifying the step size, we specify how many evenly spaced values we want between two endpoints.

00:00:26.477 --> 00:00:30.022
Here we create five values between zero and one.

00:00:30.022 --> 00:00:37.567
Zeros and ones are useful when we need an array with a known shape before filling it with

00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:39.292
other values.

00:00:39.292 --> 00:00:45.647
For example, N P dot zeros with shape two by three creates two rows and three columns,

00:00:45.647 --> 00:00:48.947
all initialized to zero. Click Run and compare the four results.

00:00:48.947 --> 00:00:56.857
These creation functions appear frequently in simulations, model initialization, time grids, thresholds,

00:00:56.857 --> 00:01:04.487
matrices, and test data. The important distinction is this: arange is usually about step size,

00:01:04.487 --> 00:01:09.237
while linspace is usually about a requested number of evenly spaced points.
